EN.push({id:"ajouts",n:"Added questions",s:"Additional questions from your CSV file, in file order.",q:[
["How do you build a delegate scorecard that is genuinely useful?",
"Combine quantitative indicators, qualitative findings, risk-based rating and dated corrective actions.",
"First define the inherent risk of the mandate: strategy, illiquidity, leverage, geography, complexity, volume and criticality of the delegate. Then select observable indicators: timeliness and quality of reporting, breaches, incidents, performance against plan and a relevant benchmark, valuation quality, conflicts, team turnover, audit results and responsiveness to information requests. For each indicator define a source, frequency, threshold, owner and escalation rule. An overall score must not hide a critical incident: some events trigger escalation regardless of the score. Document data limitations and actions, owners and deadlines."],
["How do you distinguish KPI, KRI and scorecard in the PMO?",
"A KPI measures the performance or efficiency of a process; a KRI signals a risk exposure; the scorecard aggregates the indicators and the overall judgement on the delegate.",
"KPI examples: share of reports received on time, request turnaround time, share of complete investment files. KRI examples: number and severity of breaches, concentration close to a limit, valuation delays, key person departures or control incidents. The scorecard combines trends, thresholds, materiality and qualitative assessment to decide the level of monitoring. Avoid indicators that are easy to count but not very informative, and link each measure to a possible action."],
["How do you measure the performance of a portfolio of alternative strategies at ManCo level?",
"Compare results with the objectives and risks specific to each strategy, then aggregate exposures without imposing a single benchmark on heterogeneous assets.",
"Build a dashboard by fund and strategy: gross/net return, realised/unrealised, NAV change, flows, exposure, leverage, liquidity, concentration, compliance with budget and limits, and specific risks. For PE/VC, track TVPI, DPI, RVPI and IRR with a PME where relevant; for debt, current yield, losses, defaults, spreads and credit quality; for real estate, NOI, occupancy, rents, cap rates and LTV. Distinguish absolute performance, relative performance and risk taken. Present the variances, their causes, the reliability of valuations and the actions decided."],
["How do you analyse underperformance without confusing outcome and quality of management?",
"Break the gap down by factor, horizon, valuations and risks, then assess the manager's response rather than concluding from a single figure.",
"Compare with the business plan, the relevant benchmark and the vintage or comparable universe, taking into account flows, currency, fees and the fund's life stage. Distinguish market factors, asset selection, leverage, operational execution, exit timing and changes in valuation assumptions. Check whether the underperformance is temporary or structural, whether forecasts were revised credibly and whether corrective measures are followed up. Early IRRs and unrealised NAVs can be misleading."],
["What should you do if performance looks good but relies on unreliable valuations?",
"Treat valuation quality as a risk in its own right and escalate even if published returns are favourable.",
"Compare value movements with operating results, comparable transactions, financing rounds, covenants, rates and recent events. Examine the assumptions, the date of the data, the independence of the valuer, overrides and back-testing. Identify the potential impact on NAV, performance fees, subscriptions/redemptions and investor information. Request a revaluation or independent review if necessary, then document the decision and any correction under the applicable policy."],
["How do you challenge the valuation of a private equity holding?",
"Triangulate several methods and check that assumptions are consistent with recent data and the economic rights of the instrument.",
"Analyse normalised EBITDA, comparable company and transaction multiples, net debt, non-recurring items, growth, margins and liquidity discounts if justified. For a DCF, challenge the cash flows, WACC, terminal growth and sensitivities. Reconcile enterprise value to equity value, then account for dilutive instruments, liquidation preferences and the waterfall. A recent transaction is not automatically current fair value: examine its terms, whether it was orderly, and what has changed since."],
["How do you value a start-up after a financing round at a preferential price?",
"Do not mechanically apply the last share price to all classes; analyse the rights, preferences and terms of the round.",
"Check whether the round is orderly, independent and recent enough, and whether investors benefit from preferential rights, guarantees or anti-dilution clauses. Model the distribution of value between share classes under different exit scenarios, for example with a probability-weighted scenario method or an option-pricing method where relevant. Consider dilution, runway, missed targets and market changes. Document the data used, the method, the sensitivities and the reasons why the round price is or is not representative of fair value."],
["How do you challenge a real estate valuation?",
"Examine sustainable income, market assumptions and asset characteristics, then test the sensitivity of the result.",
"Review contractual and market rents, vacancy, incentives, arrears, non-recoverable costs, capex, works schedule, lease length, tenant quality and concentration. For the capitalisation method, check the stabilised NOI and the cap rate; for the DCF, check the cash flows, explicit period, discount rate and terminal value. Compare with market transactions and yields, check the valuer's assumptions and quantify the impact of a change in rates or NOI. An external report does not relieve the AIFM of its own challenge."],
["What specific controls apply to private debt valuations?",
"Link the loan's value to the borrower's solvency, contractual cash flows, market rates and expected credit losses.",
"Check the contractual rate, market spreads, maturities, repayments, collateral, ranking, covenants, restructurings and payment delays. Estimate expected cash flows under default and recovery scenarios, discounted at a rate consistent with market conditions and risk. For distressed loans, challenge the value of collateral, legal ranking, timing and enforcement costs. Distinguish the fair value of the loan from the accounting measure of expected credit losses where the accounting framework requires it."],
["How do you monitor a venture capital portfolio beyond IRR?",
"Track the survival and progress of the companies, runway, future rounds, dilution and likely distributions.",
"Useful indicators: cash runway, burn multiple, recurring revenue growth, retention, gross margin, acquisition cost, customer concentration, product/regulatory milestones and funding needs. At fund level: reserves for follow-ons, loss rate, up/down rounds, dilution, concentration by vintage and sector, DPI/TVPI and changes in exit assumptions. Metrics must fit the stage of maturity; an isolated growth indicator is not proof of value creation."],
["How do you analyse the performance of a private equity fund of funds?",
"Analyse aggregate and underlying returns, vintages, diversification, layered fees and unfunded commitments.",
"Track IRR, TVPI, DPI and RVPI at fund and underlying fund level, with attribution by GP, vintage, strategy, sector and currency. Examine NAV lags, the share of unrealised value, distributions, two-level fees and the pace of calls. Compare with benchmarks of the same vintage and strategy and, if available, a PME. Test liquidity needs and unfunded commitments under low-distribution, high-call scenarios."],
["What specific risks do GP-led secondary funds present?",
"The main risks are the GP's conflict of interest, the transfer price, asset selection and the concentration of prolonged exposure.",
"Assess the independence of the sale process, the quality of competing bids, the justification of the price, reset fees and carried interest, the assets retained and those left out, and the options offered to existing LPs. Analyse the assets' track record, capital needs, value creation plan and governance of the continuation vehicle. Check that conflicts are identified, managed and disclosed in line with the documents and applicable obligations."],
["Which indicators signal deterioration in a private debt portfolio?",
"Monitor interest coverage, leverage, borrower liquidity, delays, amendments and covenant trends.",
"Examine net debt/EBITDA, EBITDA/interest, cash generation, available liquidity, debt maturity profile, covenant headroom, payment delays, rising PIK, amend-and-extend and waiver requests. Also track internal rating downgrades, sector deterioration and collateral value. A single amendment does not necessarily indicate default, but repeated concessions or PIK used to artificially preserve payments can signal stress. Link the signals to loss and recovery scenarios."],
["How do you assess the robustness of a real estate asset's business plan?",
"Compare the plan with historical data and the market, check operating assumptions and test adverse scenarios.",
"Review the re-letting timetable, market rents, occupancy, incentives, capex, cost inflation, permits, financing, refinancing and sale timing. Build base, downside and severe scenarios, then measure their impact on NOI, value, LTV, interest cover and cash needs. Identify the assumptions that explain most of the value creation and the milestones the asset manager controls. Update the plan when variances persist."],
["How do you monitor refinancing risk in a real estate or debt portfolio?",
"Map maturities, coverage ratios and realistic refinancing options, with adverse market scenarios.",
"Maintain a calendar of maturities, amortisations, covenants, rate hedging periods and exit dates. Test refinancing at higher rates and margins, with lower asset values and tighter LTV/LTC. Check cash sweeps, early repayments, hedges and alternatives: equity injection, partial sale, extension or restructuring. Escalate near-term maturities with no credible solution and follow the borrower's or manager's decisions."],
["How do you handle a breach of an investment limit?",
"Qualify the breach immediately, protect investors, establish the causes and apply the planned escalation and remediation procedure.",
"Confirm the facts and the data source, distinguish a passive breach caused by markets/flows from an active breach resulting from a decision, then check the applicable rules and cure periods. Inform the relevant functions according to materiality, preserve evidence and assess the impact on the fund and investors. Define a corrective plan with an owner and deadline, check its execution and analyse the root cause. Do not assume a cure period exists: refer to the fund's texts and documents."],
["How do you determine when to escalate a problem to the Conducting Officer or the board?",
"Escalate according to materiality, urgency, investor/regulatory impact, recurrence and the delegate's ability to remedy the problem.",
"Triggers can include a limit breach, a suspected valuation error, a liquidity incident, a default, an unmanaged conflict, refused information, a control weakness or a regulatory delay. Define thresholds and deadlines in the procedures, without delaying the escalation of a serious event until the periodic report. Present confirmed facts, uncertainties, potential impact, immediate measures, decision options and the follow-up owner. Keep an audit trail of decisions."],
["How do you demonstrate that the AIFM retains effective decision-making capacity when it delegates?",
"Show that the AIFM has competent resources, sufficient information and a real power to challenge, instruct and withdraw.",
"The proof does not lie only in the contract or the minutes. It includes independent analysis of proposals, documented questions, decisions modified or refused where appropriate, effective access to data, monitoring of limits and incidents, and adequate resources. The AIFM must understand the risks and be able to act in the funds' interest. A high approval rate is not automatically a problem, but must be supported by evidence of genuine review."],
["What should you check before approving a change of key person at a delegate?",
"Assess strategy continuity, the replacement's skills, operational dependencies and the funds' contractual rights.",
"Check the key person clauses, notification/consent, any suspension period and the consequences set out in the LPA or IMA. Examine the replacement's experience, team stability, allocation of responsibilities, ongoing investments and succession plans. Assess whether enhanced monitoring, a limit on new investments or investor approval is required by the documents. Document the analysis and the follow-up of the delegate's commitments."],
["How do you control fees and avoid two-level return erosion?",
"Reconcile the fees charged with the documents, calculation bases and allocation rules, then analyse their net effect on performance.",
"For funds of funds, distinguish vehicle fees, underlying fund fees, carried interest, transaction fees, monitoring fees, administration fees and any offsets. Check rates, base, period, high-water mark/hurdle and waterfall rules. Compare budgeted, booked and disclosed fees, and test allocations between compartments/investors. Present gross and net returns without double counting costs, and explain significant variances."],
["How do you review subscription lines and NAV facilities at PMO level?",
"Assess their purpose, cost, effect on published metrics, security and repayment capacity.",
"Review amount, term, total cost, covenants, guarantees, bank concentration, repayment mechanism and approval governance. Subscription lines can improve IRR by deferring capital calls without necessarily improving the economic multiple. NAV facilities create leverage secured on the portfolio value and can expose investors to refinancing or forced-sale risk. Require transparent presentation of returns with and without leverage where relevant and monitor contractual limits."],
["How do you control opportunity allocations between several funds managed by the same group?",
"Apply a pre-established, consistent and verifiable allocation policy, taking into account mandates, capacity, conflicts and investor interests.",
"Check the allocation criteria defined before the transaction: strategy, size, liquidity, diversification, available capacity, restrictions, concentration and treatment of partial opportunities. Control exceptions, co-investments, cross trades and any price or fee differences. The decision must be documented and reviewed by independent functions where required. One fund's constraints must not be circumvented to favour another vehicle of the group."],
["What should a remediation plan contain after an audit or control finding?",
"A root cause, an associated risk, a concrete corrective action, an owner, a deadline and evidence of closure.",
"Avoid vague answers such as \"raise team awareness\". Identify whether the problem stems from governance, data, the contract, the system or the control. Define a preventive action and, if necessary, an immediate compensating measure. Assign an owner and a realistic date, track delays, test effectiveness before closure and inform the competent committee of critical or recurring findings. Closure must rest on evidence, not only on the delegate's statement."],
["How do you use generative AI in the PMO without compromising confidentiality and control?",
"Use it as an assistant to summarise, compare and detect anomalies, with approved tools, human validation and traceability.",
"Do not enter confidential information in an unauthorised tool. Define permitted data, access controls, retention, leakage risks and conditions of use. For report summaries, check each finding against the source; for KPI extraction, reconcile the results with the original data; for regulatory texts, check the version and the jurisdiction. AI must neither take an investment decision alone nor replace regulatory judgement. Test for bias, hallucinations and reproducibility, then document the limits."],
["How do you assess the quality of a delegate's reporting before using it for a decision?",
"Check completeness, consistency, timeliness, reconciliations, metric definitions and audit trail.",
"Compare the figures with previous periods, NAV, accounting, depositary/administrator data and portfolio events. Check definitions, units, currencies, valuation dates, estimated data and later corrections. Spot missing data, methodology changes and unexplained variances. Unreliable data must be flagged as such and not presented to the committee as established fact."],
["What are the oversight differences between a UCITS and a private markets AIF?",
"Governance and delegation principles are common, but the liquidity profile, investment constraints, valuation and redemption rights differ.",
"For a UCITS, oversight must cover UCITS limits, asset liquidity, NAV calculation frequency, diversification rules and retail investor protection. For a private AIF, topics may include unfunded commitments, periodic valuations, capital calls, leverage, defaults, LPA restrictions and limited liquidity. Adapt frequency, indicators and escalation to the fund's risk and obligations; do not automatically transpose the same thresholds to both regimes."],
["How do you prepare for a CSSF meeting on a delegated investment file?",
"Rebuild an end-to-end audit trail and demonstrate the AIFM's own judgement, from sourcing to post-investment monitoring.",
"Present the thesis, the compliance analysis, the due diligences, the financial assumptions, the risks and scenarios, the conflicts, the opinions of the control functions, the AIFM's challenge and the formal decision. Then show the follow-up of the business plan, variances, valuations, breaches and escalations. The documents must be consistent with each other and quickly available. If a weakness is identified, explain it frankly with its impact, the measures taken and the evidence of remediation."],
["How do you analyse concentration risk when exposures are held indirectly through funds?",
"Combine direct data and look-through data where available, document the limits of visibility and avoid false precision.",
"Map exposures by issuer/borrower, group, sector, country, currency, sponsor, bank and risk factors, depending on the strategy. In underlying funds, use the most recent look-through and identify delayed or unavailable data. Define a prudent estimation method and flag potentially duplicated or correlated exposures. Compare with regulatory, contractual and internal limits, and escalate significant concentrations even when they result from indirect aggregation."],
["Which indicators should you track for a life annuity (viager) portfolio?",
"Track demographic assumptions, actually observed flows, property value and expected holding period.",
"Compare actual and expected mortality by cohort, age, sex and other relevant actuarial variables, respecting applicable data rules. Track estimated residual life/WALE, annuities paid, assumption revisions, property value, costs, works and vacant-possession costs. Results must be analysed on sufficiently large cohorts: a few deaths or absences of deaths are not enough to conclude on the quality of the model. Test the effects of longer longevity on flows, liquidity and fair value."],
["How do you monitor unfunded commitments and the liquidity needs of a private equity fund?",
"Combine available commitments, foreseeable calls, fees, debt, expected distributions and delayed-exit scenarios.",
"Maintain a rolling projection of capital calls, follow-on investments, fees, interest and other obligations. Do not treat expected distributions as certain; include a downside scenario with accelerated calls and reduced or delayed distributions. Check subscription lines, call notice periods, investor default clauses and the consequences of non-payment. Present the liquidity headroom, the pacing assumptions and the measures available if the scenario worsens."],
["How do you assess whether a benchmark is suitable for an alternative fund?",
"The benchmark must be consistent with the strategy, vintage, geography, currency, leverage and investment horizon.",
"Specify whether it is a market benchmark, a peer group or an absolute target. Listed indices can be useful as a PME to compare the timing of flows, but do not reproduce the illiquidity, leverage or smoothed valuations of private assets. Compare close vintages and strategies, explain methodological differences and do not choose the most favourable index after the fact. The benchmark complements, but does not replace, the analysis of risk and the investment plan."],
["How do you check consistency between PPM/LPA, delegation agreement and actual practice?",
"Build an obligations matrix and compare the legal documents with the controls, systems, reports and decisions observed.",
"Compare objectives, investment universe, limits, leverage, liquidity, valuation, fees, conflicts, decision-making powers, reporting, sub-delegation and investor rights. Test a sample of transactions and reports to check actual application of the rules. Every gap must be qualified: a mere documentation error, a control weakness or a potentially non-compliant practice. Define the owner of the fix, the need for legal advice and, if necessary, notification or information to investors/authorities."],
["How do you judge whether the monitoring frequency is proportionate to the risk?",
"Apply a risk-based approach, complemented by event-driven reviews and minimum regulatory or contractual requirements.",
"Consider asset materiality, illiquidity, leverage, complexity, incident history, data quality, team stability and the criticality of the mandate. A high-risk delegate may require monthly indicators, more frequent meetings and targeted controls; a stable profile may justify a lower frequency, without removing essential controls. Review the classification after an incident, an acquisition, a team change or a deterioration in performance. Document the rationale and approvals."],
["How do you react if the delegate and the AIFM disagree on a valuation?",
"Follow the independent valuation governance, examine the evidence and document the final decision under the applicable policy.",
"Request the delegate's source data, assumptions and methods, then conduct an independent challenge with the valuation function and, as the case may be, Risk, the Valuation Committee or an external expert. Quantify the impact on NAV, fees and investors. Distinguish a methodological disagreement from a factual error or a conflict of interest. Do not let the manager concerned impose its own value alone; apply the fund's policy, document dissenting opinions and escalate material cases."],
["How do you ensure effective oversight of US delegates from Luxembourg?",
"Establish effective access and control rights, a cooperation framework and an understanding of legal and operational differences.",
"Examine the delegate's regulatory status, the exact scope of the mandate, data availability, audit/visit rights, notification obligations and cooperation between authorities where required. Assess time zone, group system dependency, sub-delegation, confidentiality and continuity risks. Provide for named contacts, response times, common reporting formats and cross-border escalation. A non-EU location does not automatically prohibit delegation, but increases the importance of demonstrating effective access and control."],
["What evidence demonstrates continuous improvement of the PMO?",
"Before/after indicators, a reduction in manual tasks and delays, better risk coverage and findings durably closed.",
"Measure, for example, the share of reviews completed on time, file completeness, time spent on collection, data exceptions, action delays and recurrence of findings. Show that automation frees time for judgement rather than removing controls. Keep change governance, tests, user validation, documentation and the fallback plan. Effectiveness is measured by the quality of decisions and risk reduction, not only by the number of hours saved."],
["[PRIORITY 1 — OVERSIGHT] How do you demonstrate that the AIFM exercises real oversight and does not just collect the delegate's reports?",
"Oversight must combine independent analysis, documented challenge, decisions and action follow-up. Receiving a report is not enough.",
"In the interview, structure your answer around five elements: (1) understand the mandate, strategy, risks and applicable limits; (2) check the quality and consistency of the information received against other available sources; (3) challenge variances, assumptions, valuations and significant decisions; (4) record the questions, answers, conclusions, decisions and owners; (5) follow actions through to closure and escalate delays or material disagreements. Give a concrete, anonymised example. The AIFM retains its regulatory responsibilities even when a function is delegated. Background reference: Article 20 AIFMD and the Luxembourg law of 12 July 2013; the principles must be applied to BEFM's specific set-up."],
["[PRIORITY 1 — OVERSIGHT] How do you design a delegate scorecard that is truly risk-based?",
"I would build a balanced scorecard, with quantitative and qualitative indicators, thresholds defined in advance, weighting adapted to the delegate's profile and actions tied to each alert level.",
"Possible dimensions: performance and compliance with the mandate; compliance with limits and investment restrictions; quality and timeliness of reporting; valuation and justification of value changes; liquidity, leverage and concentrations; operational and compliance incidents; team stability and resources; cooperation with the AIFM; quality of remediation. Each KPI/KRI must have a definition, source, frequency, owner, threshold, tolerance and escalation procedure. Avoid an overall score that masks a critical weakness: some events must trigger escalation regardless of the score. Calibrate the review frequency according to materiality, complexity, incident history and the level of delegation."],
["[PRIORITY 1 — OVERSIGHT] What difference do you make between KPI, KRI, control and scorecard?",
"A KPI measures the performance of a process or an outcome; a KRI signals an exposure or a deterioration in risk; a control is an action designed to prevent or detect a problem; the scorecard brings several elements together into a structured assessment.",
"Example: the rate of reports received on time is an operational KPI; the number of limit breaches or the frequency of valuation adjustments can be a KRI; the independent reconciliation of exposures with limits is a control; the scorecard brings together this data, the qualitative findings and the follow-up of actions. In the interview, explain how the indicators lead to a decision, and not just to a presentation. Define thresholds and responsibilities before observing the results, to avoid a retrospective or subjective assessment."],
["[PRIORITY 1 — OVERSIGHT] What do you do if an Investment Manager disputes your conclusion or refuses to implement a corrective action?",
"I clarify the facts and applicable requirements, document the respective positions, seek a resolution within a defined timeframe and escalate according to materiality, urgency and BEFM's governance.",
"Start by distinguishing a difference of analysis from a breach of a limit, procedure or obligation. Check the data and the contract/mandate, state the risk precisely and ask for a supported response. Set an owner and a deadline. If the risk is material, imminent, repeated or tied to investors' interests, inform the relevant functions and the Conducting Officer without delay under internal procedures; involve Risk, Compliance, Legal or Valuation depending on the subject. An action must not be closed on the delegate's promise alone: require evidence of implementation and test the effectiveness of the remedy."],
["[PRIORITY 1 — OVERSIGHT] How do you determine the frequency and depth of a delegate's due diligence?",
"I apply an approach proportionate to inherent and residual risks, taking into account strategy, complexity, size, geography, sub-delegation, incidents and the quality of existing control.",
"An initial review should cover in particular authorisation and supervision, ownership and governance, resources, experience, conflicts of interest, the risk and compliance framework, valuation, operational controls, business continuity, cybersecurity and the quality of reporting. The periodic review can be modulated by risk, but must be complemented by trigger events: change of control, departure of key people, modified strategy or operating model, incident, persistent underperformance, disputed valuation or financial deterioration. Document the rationale for the frequency and the changes since the previous review."],
["[PRIORITY 1 — OVERSIGHT] What evidence should you keep to demonstrate effective oversight during a CSSF inspection?",
"A traceable file linking identified risks to the analyses carried out, the challenges raised, the decisions taken, the escalations and the verified closure of actions.",
"Prepare an audit trail comprising: inventory of delegates and delegated scope; risk analysis and due diligence calendar; questionnaires and supporting documents; scorecards and source data; minutes or review notes; questions sent to the delegate and answers received; breaches and incidents; governance decisions; action plans, deadlines and evidence of closure; justification of exceptions and delays. The evidence must allow an independent person to reconstruct what was known, when, by whom, and why a decision was taken. Quality documentation does not replace judgement, but it must reflect genuine challenge work."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] How do you analyse the underperformance of a private equity fund?",
"I break performance down into change in holdings' value, operating growth, margins, debt, valuation multiples, currency, fees and exit timing, then compare the results with the business plan and relevant references.",
"For a PE fund, look in particular at TVPI, DPI, RVPI, net and gross IRR, entry and exit multiples, revenue and EBITDA growth, margin, cash conversion, leverage at holding level and holding period. Distinguish realised from unrealised performance and analyse sensitivity to residual value. Compare with vintage, strategy, region and sector, bearing in mind that benchmarks are not always perfectly comparable. Challenge variances against plan, the frequency of write-ups/write-downs, exit delays and dependence on a few holdings. Do not conclude that a high IRR alone proves portfolio quality: the timing of flows can strongly influence this indicator."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] Which KPIs should you use for a private equity fund of funds?",
"I combine the fund of funds' return metrics with a look-through analysis of the underlying funds, concentrations, liquidity and valuation quality.",
"At fund level: net IRR, TVPI, DPI, RVPI, cash flows, capital calls, distributions, unfunded commitments, two-level fees and costs, currency exposure and cash forecasts. At look-through level: exposure by GP, vintage, strategy, sector, country, currency and underlying holdings where data is available. Monitor concentration with the largest GPs, reporting delays, stale valuations, distributions below expectations and liquidity needs linked to unfunded commitments. Explain the limits of look-through data and how you treat missing or lagged information."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] Which KPIs are most useful for a venture capital portfolio?",
"I examine the fund's returns and flows, but also the companies' operating indicators, cash runway, future funding needs, down rounds and dilution risk.",
"At fund level: IRR, TVPI, DPI, RVPI, available reserves, unfunded commitments and concentration. At company level: revenue growth, gross margin, burn rate, runway, customer retention depending on the model, acquisition cost, dependence on external funding and operating milestones. Analyse recent financing rounds, preferential rights, capital structure, convertible instruments and dilution effects. Comparisons must take into account stage (seed, Series A, growth), vintage and sector. Valuations may rely on recent rounds, but an old round, or one done under different conditions, is not automatically current fair value."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] How do you monitor a private debt portfolio?",
"I monitor yield and cash collections, but also credit quality, debt service capacity, covenants, collateral, concentration, payment delays and expected losses.",
"Possible KPIs/KRIs: current yield and yield to maturity, interest income received versus expected, PIK, delays and defaults, watchlist, internal rating, borrower leverage, interest coverage, net debt/EBITDA, covenant headroom, LTV, collateral coverage and ranking, expected recovery, restructurings, concentration by borrower/sector/country and maturity profile. For distressed loans, challenge recovery assumptions, collateral enforcement costs and timing, and enterprise value. Distinguish income recognised from cash actually received and examine capitalised interest, which can mask deterioration."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] Which indicators should you track for a real estate portfolio?",
"I link operating real estate indicators to cash flows, valuation, debt and the business plan, by asset and at portfolio level.",
"Indicators: occupancy rate, contractual and collected rents, vacancy, arrears, WAULT/WALE of leases depending on the definition used, expiries and breaks, reversion, NOI, operating margin, capex, renovation costs, rental yield, value per sq m, cap rate, LTV, debt service coverage and debt maturities. Compare actual, budget, last valuation and downside scenario. Analyse assets under works separately from stabilised assets. Occupancy alone can be misleading if rents are below market, if large incentives have been granted or if operating costs are rising."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] How do you assess the performance of a hedge fund or a liquid strategy?",
"I compare return, risk, liquidity and compliance with the mandate, taking into account net/gross exposure, leverage, drawdowns, risk factors and the ability to meet redemptions.",
"Depending on the strategy, track net-of-fee return, volatility, maximum drawdown, Sharpe/Sortino, gross and net exposure, concentrations, market beta, factors, turnover, position liquidity, counterparties, margins and collateral calls. Check that the metrics suit the strategy: Sharpe can be misleading for non-normal returns or infrequently valued assets. Compare with objectives, limits and a relevant benchmark. Test performance under stress and liquidation times, rather than limiting yourself to monthly return."],
["[PRIORITY 2 — PERFORMANCE] How do you distinguish economic underperformance from a valuation or timing effect?",
"I reconcile published performance with cash flows, value changes, valuation assumptions, operating events and comparables, then explain realised and unrealised effects separately.",
"Reconstruct the NAV change: inflows/outflows, income, costs, realised gains/losses, revaluations, currency and other adjustments. Check whether values are contemporaneous, whether assumptions have changed and whether comparables are relevant. Analyse the asset's operating indicators and realisations after the valuation date where available. A stable NAV does not necessarily imply stable risk, especially for illiquid assets. Document the uncertainty and data limitations, without claiming that an estimated value is a certain exit price."],
["[PRIORITY 3 — VALUATION] How do you challenge a private equity valuation based on an EBITDA multiple?",
"I challenge the quality of EBITDA, the choice of comparables, the multiple used, net debt, fair value adjustments and consistency with market conditions at the measurement date.",
"Check recent financial results and the period used, exceptional items and unrealised synergies, EBITDA normalisation, the selection of listed comparables or comparable transactions, and differences in growth, margin, size and risk. Examine the bridge from enterprise value to equity value: debt, cash, debt-like items, preferred instruments and dilution. Test sensitivity to the multiple and EBITDA and compare with recent financing rounds or market offers where relevant. The method must reflect fair value at the measurement date, not simply roll forward the acquisition price."],
["[PRIORITY 3 — VALUATION] How do you challenge the valuation of a real estate asset?",
"I check the method, rental data, NOI, cap or discount rates, comparables, capex, vacancy assumptions and consistency with the market and the business plan.",
"Examine the valuer's independence and competence, the valuation date, the instructions and any conflicts. Test the main assumptions: market rents, vacancy, incentives, costs, works, exit rate, discount rate and stabilisation timetable. Compare value movements with those of comparable assets and explain significant changes. Identify valuations that rely on highly sensitive assumptions, old transactions or insufficient data. For development assets, challenge the remaining cost to complete, timelines, permits and contingencies. Document disagreements and their resolution."],
["[PRIORITY 3 — VALUATION] How do you value or challenge a deteriorating private debt loan?",
"I combine the analysis of contractual flows and credit risk with default and recovery scenarios, examining collateral, seniority, covenants and market conditions.",
"Methods may include discounting expected cash flows at a rate reflecting the appropriate market risk, credit comparables or a recovery approach depending on the circumstances and the applicable policy. Check probabilities of default, loss given default, recovery timing, legal costs, debt ranking, collateral value and liquidity, guarantees and any restructurings. Distinguish the accounting provision or IFRS ECL, where applicable, from the fair value used for the NAV: they serve different purposes and frameworks and must not be automatically conflated."],
["[PRIORITY 3 — VALUATION] How do you handle valuations of illiquid assets when information is old or incomplete?",
"I measure the risk linked to the age and quality of the data, look for updated evidence, run sensitivity analyses and escalate material uncertainties under the valuation governance.",
"Assess the date of the last report, significant events since that date, the availability of recent operating data, comparable transactions, changes in rates and spreads, and subsequent financings or sales. A reporting lag must be identified and treated consistently; it does not automatically justify an arbitrary adjustment. Document the sources, assumptions, limits and potential NAV impact. Provide for an independent challenge process, materiality thresholds and escalation in case of disagreement. The CSSF has kept the valuation of less liquid and illiquid assets among its supervisory priorities for 2026."],
["[PRIORITY 3 — VALUATION] What is the difference between the AIFM's responsibility and that of an external valuer?",
"Using an external valuer does not transfer the AIFM's regulatory responsibility for the proper valuation of assets and the calculation of the NAV.",
"The AIFM must have appropriate valuation policies and procedures, ensure consistent application, manage conflicts of interest and challenge the results. Under the AIFMD, the AIFM's responsibility for asset valuation, NAV calculation and its publication is not removed by appointing an external valuer. In the interview, distinguish producing the valuation, controlling it and approving it under the applicable governance. Do not confuse the valuer's independence, the independence of the valuation function and the role of Portfolio Oversight: the precise responsibilities must be described in line with BEFM's organisation and procedures."],
["[PRIORITY 4 — CSSF] Which 2026 CSSF priorities are particularly relevant for a Portfolio Oversight Manager?",
"The supervision of delegates and third-party providers, the robustness of the risk framework, the valuation of illiquid assets, governance and ICT/cyber risks are particularly relevant.",
"In 2026 the CSSF announced increased attention to third-party and delegation risks, in line with the ESMA principles on third-party risk and CSSF Circular 18/698. The valuation of less liquid/illiquid assets remains a priority, notably for private asset funds and continuation funds. In July 2026 the CSSF also launched a joint ESMA/CSSF supervisory action on the risk management function of UCITS ManCos and AIFMs. Link these themes to operational elements: inventory and classification of delegates, risk-based controls, challenge of valuations, follow-up of corrective actions, independence and resources of the risk function, reporting to management and incident governance. References: CSSF 2026 supervisory priorities; communication of 4 June 2026 on valuation; communication of 27 July 2026 on the Risk Management CSA."],
["[PRIORITY 4 — CSSF] What does the CSSF thematic review teach you about the delegation of portfolio management?",
"It reminds us that an oversight framework must be effective, documented, proportionate to risks and regularly assessed, and that you must be able to demonstrate how the AIFM actually controls the delegated functions.",
"Be ready to explain how you assess the delegate before its appointment, monitor its activities on an ongoing basis, identify incidents and conflicts of interest, follow the quality of reporting, challenge decisions and remedy weaknesses. In October 2024 the CSSF published the conclusions of its thematic review on the delegation of portfolio management and invited IFMs to assess their framework in light of the observations. For the interview, do not claim to know every finding without having read the full report: retain the substantive expectations and prepare examples of evidence and corrective actions. Source: CSSF, communication of 23 October 2024."],
["[PRIORITY 4 — CSSF] How do you distinguish portfolio management oversight from the independent risk management function?",
"Portfolio Oversight controls and challenges the delegates' activities within its scope, while the Risk Management function must keep the independence, authority and resources required to identify, measure and monitor risks under the applicable framework.",
"The exact split depends on BEFM's governance, but responsibilities must be clear, without gaps or inefficient duplication. PMO can examine compliance with the mandate, results, limits, incidents and data quality; Risk Management carries out its independent analyses and controls under its mandate. The functions must share relevant information, coordinate escalations and avoid an operational function validating itself. Prepare an example of market, credit or liquidity risk where both functions intervene with distinct responsibilities. In July 2026 the CSSF/ESMA launched a CSA covering in particular the governance, independence, resources, risk measurement and reporting of the risk function."],
["[PRIORITY 4 — CSSF] What do you need to know about AIFMD II for this interview?",
"I must understand the changes affecting delegation, reporting on delegation arrangements, substance, liquidity management for certain open-ended funds and AIF-originated loans, then check their transposition and implementation as applicable to BEFM.",
"AIFMD II is Directive (EU) 2024/927. Among other things, it strengthens information on delegates, delegated functions, the resources employed and the oversight arrangements. It also contains provisions on liquidity management tools, loan-originating funds and other topics. For a solid answer, avoid reciting the directive without distinction: identify the type of fund (AIF or UCITS), the strategy, BEFM's role, the delegate's status and the locally transposed rules. Check the application dates and the relevant Luxembourg measures before the interview; do not present all provisions as already applying identically to every fund."],
["[PRIORITY 5 — KPI] How do you set relevant alert thresholds without generating too many false positives?",
"I combine regulatory and contractual limits with internal thresholds calibrated on history, materiality, data volatility and risk appetite, then review their relevance periodically.",
"Distinguish prospectus/mandate limits, regulatory limits, early warning thresholds and escalation thresholds. For each indicator, define a source, frequency, tolerance, threshold, owner and expected action. Use trends and relative thresholds when they are more informative than an absolute threshold. Analyse false positives and missed alerts. Do not relax a threshold just because it produces many alerts: first look for the cause, the data quality and the associated control. Exceptions must be approved, justified and traced."],
["[PRIORITY 5 — KPI] How do you measure the quality of the PMO function itself?",
"I measure the coverage and timeliness of controls, the quality of challenge, the resolution of problems and the usefulness of reporting for decision-making.",
"Examples: due diligences completed on time; complete periodic reviews; delegates covered by an up-to-date scorecard; reports received and analysed on time; incidents detected and escalated on time; overdue corrective actions; average closure time; recurrence rate of findings; data quality; number of recurring findings; committee decisions taken on the basis of PMO analyses. Do not target only the number of controls or a low number of incidents: this can encourage a façade compliance approach. Complement the metrics with qualitative reviews of samples and stakeholder feedback."],
["[PRIORITY 5 — KPI] How do you monitor valuations in a fund of funds portfolio?",
"I monitor the freshness, consistency and reconciliation of underlying values, timing differences, unusual movements and concentration in funds or managers whose valuations are less transparent.",
"Possible indicators: age of the last underlying NAV; proportion of exposures valued with lagged data; manual adjustments; unexplained NAV movements; differences between NAV received and NAV booked; reporting delays; exposure by manager, vintage and strategy; share of value based on estimates or incomplete data. An underlying valuation can be correct within its own framework yet too old for the investing fund's needs. Set up a process to identify significant subsequent events, document any adjustments and avoid double counting."],
["[PRIORITY 6 — CASE STUDY] You discover that an investment limit has been exceeded. What is your reaction?",
"I quickly confirm the facts, qualify the nature and materiality of the breach, check the applicable rules, alert the competent functions and bodies according to the procedure, then follow the remediation and prevention of recurrence.",
"Steps: (1) validate the data and the calculation; (2) determine whether the breach is passive or active, temporary or persistent, regulatory or contractual, and identify the causes; (3) assess the impact on the fund and investors; (4) inform the appropriate functions, notably Risk, Compliance, Legal and the Conducting Officer according to governance; (5) define the corrective measures, the deadline and any restrictions; (6) check whether notifications or communications are required; (7) document the incident and carry out a root cause analysis. Do not automatically impose an immediate sale: the response depends on the texts, the prospectus, investors' interest and the facts."],
["[PRIORITY 6 — CASE STUDY] The NAV of an illiquid fund suddenly falls by 15% while the market seems stable. What do you do?",
"I ask for a breakdown of the change and supporting evidence, check the data and assumptions, compare with operating indicators and the market, then escalate if the adjustment is material or insufficiently justified.",
"Examine the portfolio composition and each asset's contribution; changes in cash flows, multiples, discount rates, cap rates, credit spreads or default probabilities; operating events; recent transactions; currency movements; fees and exceptional entries. Compare the assumptions with the previous period and test sensitivity. Check who produced, challenged and approved the valuation in accordance with the policy. If the justification is insufficient, request further analysis and follow the escalation governance. Do not automatically equate a large movement with an error: it may reflect a specific event or a legitimate revision of assumptions."],
["[PRIORITY 6 — CASE STUDY] An Investment Advisor recommends an acquisition with an attractive-looking return, but the business plan assumptions are aggressive. How do you challenge it?",
"I test the key assumptions, compare the base case with downside scenarios, check consistency with the fund's strategy and limits, then clearly present the risks, conditions and items still to be confirmed.",
"Question growth, margins, timing, capex, financing need, entry price, exit multiples, rates, liquidity and refinancing assumptions. Ask for independent sources, comparables and due diligence elements. Calculate the impact on IRR/MOIC or credit yield depending on the strategy and check whether the return really compensates the risks. Identify legal, tax, operational, ESG and conflict of interest risks. Distinguish the IA's recommendation role, the committee's decisions and the AIFM's formal responsibilities. The conclusion may be approval, refusal or conditional approval depending on the facts and the governance rules."],
["[PRIORITY 6 — CASE STUDY] How do you react to a valuation that seems systematically optimistic compared with subsequent realisations?",
"I analyse the gaps between earlier valuations and realisations, look for recurring biases or assumptions, raise the level of challenge and escalate according to materiality and the valuation governance.",
"Build a retrospective analysis: value estimated at the measurement date versus sale price, refinancing or subsequent transaction, adjusting as far as possible for market changes and events between the dates. Look for persistent biases in multiples, rates, projections or provisions. Check whether negative events were known or reasonably identifiable at the valuation date. An ex post gap does not by itself prove that the initial estimate was wrong; it is nonetheless a useful signal to test the method, the data and the controls. Document the conclusions and the improvements required."],
["[PRIORITY 7 — LEADERSHIP] How do you build a \"best in class\" PMO function while keeping processes lean?",
"I standardise the essential controls, adapt their depth to risks, automate repetitive tasks and devote human judgement to exceptions, trends and material decisions.",
"Start by mapping delegates, obligations, risks and existing processes. Remove duplicates and reports with no associated decision. Create an integrated calendar, consistent scorecard templates, common KPI definitions and a centralised audit trail. Automate collection, completeness checks, historical comparisons and alerts, but keep human validation for exceptions and decisions. Steer the changes with indicators of quality, timeliness, incidents, delays and user feedback. A lean process does not mean less control: it means resources are concentrated where they really reduce risk."],
["[PRIORITY 7 — LEADERSHIP] How do you use AI and automation in the PMO without creating new risks?",
"I favour low-risk use cases with verifiable benefit, with approved data, controlled access, human validation, traceability and clear confidentiality rules.",
"Use cases: data extraction from reports, comparison of document versions, generation of a first summary, detection of missing values, preparation of a list of anomalies and action tracking. Do not load confidential or personal data into an unapproved tool. Validate calculations and citations against the original source; do not let a model alone take an investment, valuation or escalation decision. Document the limits, tests, access rights and control owner. Measure time saved and error rate. AI must assist judgement and not replace the team's responsibility."],
["[PRIORITY 7 — LEADERSHIP] How do you present a complex portfolio problem to a committee or board?",
"I start with the decision required, then present the facts, impact, options, recommendation, residual risks and actions with owners and deadlines.",
"A good committee paper must separate verified facts, assumptions, uncertainties and judgement. Use a few readable indicators, a history, a downside scenario and a comparison with the limits or the business plan. Explain what has changed, why it matters to investors, what is asked of the committee and the consequences of not deciding. Avoid overly detailed tables in the body of the document; put technical elements in an appendix. Keep a record of the decision, the reservations expressed and the actions."],
["[PRIORITY 7 — LEADERSHIP] How do you coach a junior analyst in charge of preparing delegate scorecards?",
"I define expectations and quality controls, explain the reasoning behind the indicators, review the conclusions and give concrete feedback so that they progress towards autonomous analysis.",
"Provide a standard template, authorised definitions and sources, escalation examples and a control checklist. Ask the analyst to explain significant movements, not just fill in the cells. Check data accuracy, consistency with previous periods, comments, thresholds and proposed actions. Organise regular reviews and document improvements. Delegating the preparation does not mean delegating responsibility for the final judgement or the escalation."],
["[PRIORITY 7 — LEADERSHIP] How do you manage stakeholders in the US and EMEA with different expectations?",
"I define a common base of controls and data, clarify local requirements and responsibilities, then adapt communication to the context without diluting regulatory obligations.",
"Establish common KPI definitions, a reporting calendar, data owners and escalation paths. Distinguish global group requirements from the local requirements applicable to BEFM and the funds concerned. Anticipate time zones, prepare decisions and send actions with owner and date. In case of disagreement, bring the discussion back to the facts, the mandate, the applicable texts and the interest of investors. Avoid assuming that the group process automatically replaces the AIFM's local obligations."],
["[PRIORITY 7 — LEADERSHIP] How do you handle a recurring weakness identified across several delegates?",
"I deal with both each individual case and the systemic cause, then check whether the control framework, instructions or PMO tools need to be improved.",
"Group the findings by theme, materiality, strategy and root cause. Distinguish a weakness specific to one delegate from a cross-cutting reporting, valuation or governance problem. Define proportionate actions: targeted communication, revision of the questionnaire, change to a KPI, cross-cutting control, training or governance escalation. Check closure and test whether the problem reappears. A high number of similar findings can reveal a weakness in the AIFM's own oversight framework, not only in the delegates."],
["How would you prepare a delegate file for an on-site CSSF inspection?",
"I would prepare a structured file that retraces the full cycle: selection, due diligence, contracting, ongoing monitoring, incidents, escalations, corrective actions and review of the framework's effectiveness.",
"First present the risk profile, the delegated scope, the assets concerned and the justification for the delegation. Then show the controls and their frequency, the scorecards, the evidence of challenge, the incidents and the governance decisions. Prepare a few examples where an alert led to real action, as well as a case where the AIFM concluded that no action was necessary, explaining why. Ensure consistency between procedures, practice and evidence. Identify known gaps, their owner, deadline and status; do not try to hide a weakness."],
["How do you monitor conflicts of interest between the AIFM, the delegate, the GP and investors?",
"I identify potential conflicts before and during the life of the investment, check the prevention and management measures, and make sure decisions are documented and information is disclosed where required.",
"Examples: allocation of opportunities between funds, transactions between affiliated vehicles, fees and commissions, valuation of related holdings, continuation funds, related-party transactions, GP remuneration and selection of affiliated service providers. Assess the policies, segregation of responsibilities, independent approvals and applicable disclosures. In PMO, challenge the delegate's analysis and check that the conflict is dealt with in practice, not merely entered in a register. Involve Compliance/Legal when the situation requires."],
["How do you integrate ESG and sustainability risks into the monitoring of delegates and portfolios?",
"I check consistency between the announced strategy, disclosure obligations and the delegate's actual practices, then monitor the material risks and indicators relevant for each asset class.",
"The framework depends on the fund, its sustainability characteristics or objectives and the applicable rules. Examine the delegate's policies, responsibilities, data quality, the indicators selected, controls on declarations and any controversies. For real estate, this may include energy, emissions, certifications and transition capex; for credit and PE, material governance, supply chain or transition risks depending on the activity. Do not assume that a label or SFDR classification guarantees sustainable performance. Check the disclosures and their consistency with underlying decisions and data."],
["How do you manage liquidity risk in a fund holding private assets?",
"I reconcile forecast commitments and cash needs with expected distributions, debt maturities, possible redemptions and the real ability to sell or finance the assets under a stress scenario.",
"Analyse capital calls, unfunded commitments, fees, interest, capex, maturities, guarantees, expected distributions and flow concentration. Build downside scenarios: delayed distributions, simultaneous capital calls, a borrower default, a fall in asset values, closure of the refinancing market or redemption requests under the fund's terms. Examine credit lines and their covenants, without treating an undrawn facility as a guaranteed solution. Liquidity must be analysed according to the fund's characteristics, the assets, the redemption terms and the tools legally and contractually available."],
["How do you assess leverage risk at the level of an AIF and an underlying portfolio?",
"I look at the applicable regulatory methods, the sources of leverage, asset sensitivity, covenants, collateral needs and the ability to withstand adverse scenarios.",
"Distinguish leverage calculated under the AIFMD gross and commitment methods, economic leverage at the level of the underlying assets and financing leverage. Check borrowings, derivatives, repos, guarantees and interposed structures under the applicable rules. For private equity, examine debt at company level and refinancing maturities; for real estate, LTV and debt service coverage; for liquid strategies, gross/net exposure and margin calls. A high ratio only makes sense with its calculation method, scope and context. Test shocks to rates, spreads, values and collateral."],
["How do you determine whether a benchmark is suitable for judging an Investment Manager?",
"The benchmark must be consistent with the mandate, strategy, risk, geography, vintage and liquidity; where no index is sufficiently comparable, I use complementary references and spell out their limits.",
"For PE/VC, comparisons by vintage, strategy and quartile may be more useful than a general listed index, but data is often lagged and sensitive to reporting methods. For real estate, compare similar assets and markets and take income and valuations into account. For private credit, analyse yield and losses relative to credit risk and structure. For hedge funds, choose a benchmark suited to the strategy and examine risk factors. Avoid comparisons between gross and net metrics or between different horizons."],
["What should you do if the delegate's data arrives late, is inconsistent or insufficiently granular?",
"I qualify the materiality of the gap, look for an alternative source where possible, apply plausibility checks and escalate if the quality of information prevents reliable oversight.",
"Track delays and errors as control indicators, not mere administrative irritants. Check reconciliations with the administrator, the depositary, market data or previous reports depending on the subject. Distinguish temporary estimates from confirmed data and state the limits clearly in committee reports. Set corrective actions and deadlines with the delegate. If essential information is missing, assess the impact on valuation, limit monitoring, liquidity or investors, and escalate according to the procedures."],
["How do you ensure good governance of the models and automated tools used for oversight?",
"I document the purpose, data, assumptions, tests, limits and owner of each tool, then control the results and version changes.",
"For a dashboard or script, provide for control of sources, calculation rules, access, modifications, errors and reconciliations with an independent reference. Test edge cases and keep an audit trail. Automatic outputs must be flagged as such and verified before being used for an important decision. For an AI tool, add controls for confidentiality, hallucination, bias and citations. Critical processes must have a manual fallback procedure and a clearly identified owner."]
]});
